{ Welcome to Chapter 7 of our White repertoire — mark this entry as Informational on Chessable.<br><br>When Black accepts the gambit with 2... dxc4, quiet 3. Nf3 or 3. e3 lets Black equalize comfortably with ...Nf6, ...e6, and ...c5. Instead, we seize the entire center immediately with 3. e4! — creating an asymmetrical, tactical battleground where Black must prove their compensation for our central space and rapid piece development. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e4 $1 { 3. e4! is our flagship central thrust. Rather than rushing to win the c4-pawn back, we claim e4 and d4 at once and challenge Black to attack our center immediately. } { [%csl Ge4,Gd4] [%cal Bf1c4,Ge4e5] } 3... e5 { 3... e5 is the main classical test, fighting for central space right away. Click any of Black's other major move-3 replies below to see our unified map: } ( 3... Nf6 4. e5 Nd5 5. Bxc4 Nb6 6. Bd3 { — Alekhine System: 4. e5! drives the knight to d5 and gains space before 5. Bxc4 Nb6 6. Bd3 clamps the b1-h7 diagonal.<br><br>What if Black tries to hold c4 with 3... b5?: } ) ( 3... b5 4. a4 c6 5. Nc3 b4 6. Nb1 { — Greco Variation: 4. a4! undermines the b5-c4 chain immediately, and simple 5. Nc3 b4 6. Nb1 avoids messy tactics while winning c4 back cleanly.<br><br>Against 3... Nc6?: } ) ( 3... Nc6 4. Nf3 Bg4 5. d5 Ne5 6. Bf4 { — Modern Defense: 4. Nf3 Bg4 5. d5! kicks the knight away and grabs space just like Karpov vs Milov.<br><br>And if Black strikes with 3... c5?: } ) ( 3... c5 4. d5 Nf6 5. Nc3 e6 6. Bxc4 { — Rubinstein Defense: 4. d5! clamps a Benoni-style space edge and meets ...Bd6 with 8. Qe2+! stripping castling rights. } ) 4. Nf3 exd4 5. Bxc4 Nc6 6. O-O { By move 6 we have recovered the pawn with tempo and castled safely. Across Chapter 7 you will master four repeatable blueprints:<br><br>• McDonnell Queen Raid & Bind — fracturing e6 with Bxe6 before hitting b7 and e6 with Qb3 and building b3-Ba3 pressure.<br><br>• Alekhine e5 Wedge — clamping Black's minor pieces behind d4 and e5 with Bd3, Ne2, f3, and Ne4.<br><br>• Greco Wing Undermine — challenging b5 with a4, rerouting Nc3-b1-d2, and trading off Black's active light-squared bishop with Qc2.<br><br>• d4-d5 Space Steamroller — pushing d5 against c5 and Nc6 to restrict Black's queenside and open central tactics. } { [%csl Yg1,Yc4,Ge4] [%cal Bf1e1,Bc1g5,Bb1d2] } *

