{ Black's most principled defense against 3. e4 is the McDonnell 3... e5, immediately challenging d4. After 4. Nf3 exd4 5. Bxc4 Nc6 6. O-O Be6, we skip routine 7. Bb5 and unleash 7. Bxe6! — fracturing Black's kingside pawns and launching 8. Qb3 followed by Nbd2 and 12. b3!. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e4 { 3. e4 grabs full central space before recovering c4. } { [%csl Ge4,Gd4] [%cal Bf1c4,Gd4e5] } 3... e5 { 3... e5 challenges d4 head-on and is played roughly 40% of the time in both Masters and 2000-2500 online games. Notice two rarer move-3 sidelines below — passive 3... c6 and 3... a6: } ( 3... c6 4. Bxc4 Nf6 5. Nc3 { — Passive 3... c6 neglects development and fails to challenge e4-d4; 4. Bxc4! leaves White with a huge +1.25 to +1.43 central advantage.<br><br>What if Black tries 3... a6?: } ) ( 3... a6 4. Bxc4 c5 5. d5 { — 3... a6 wastes a tempo on the flank; 4. Bxc4 recovers the pawn cleanly and meets 4... c5 with 5. d5! for +0.56. } ) 4. Nf3 { 4. Nf3 develops with tempo against e5 and keeps central tension. Autopilot 4. dxe5?? is a major mistake — after 4... Qxd1+ 5. Kxd1 Nc6, White loses castling rights and trails in development at -0.34. } { [%csl Yf3] [%cal Bf3e5,Bf3d4] } 4... exd4 { 4... exd4 opens the d-file and e-file, while 4... Bb4+ is covered in Line 1.3. Look at Black's two inaccurate alternatives here — 4... Nf6 and the premature pin 4... Bg4: } ( 4... Nf6 5. Bxc4 Bb4+ 6. Bd2 { — 4... Nf6 lets White recapture 5. Bxc4! with tempo against f7 — after 5... Bb4+ 6. Bd2, Black faces tactical pressure at +0.77.<br><br>Even worse is 4... Bg4?: } ) ( 4... Bg4 $6 5. Bxc4 Qe7 6. Nc3 { — 4... Bg4?! ignores the e5 and f7 weaknesses; 5. Bxc4! leaves Black scrambling after 5... Qe7 6. Nc3 at +2.16! } ) 5. Bxc4 { On 4... exd4, recapturing with the bishop via 5. Bxc4! is essential — it develops with tempo along the a2-g8 diagonal aiming at f7 and opens the semi-open d-file. Never play 5. Qxd4? because 5... Qxd4 6. Nxd4 Bc5 hands Black easy equality at -0.35. } { [%csl Yc4,Rf7] [%cal Bc4f7,Bc4d5] } 5... Nc6 { 5... Nc6 pressures d4 while developing. Watch three key deviations here — 5... c5, 5... Nf6, and 5... Bc5 — before we castle: } ( 5... c5 $6 6. Ne5 $1 Be6 7. Bxe6 fxe6 8. Qh5+ { — Important 4.3%-reach online trap: 5... c5?! leaves f7 fatally exposed to 6. Ne5! Be6 7. Bxe6 fxe6 8. Qh5+!, +1.48, wrecking Black's kingside.<br><br>If Black develops 5... Nf6?: } ) ( 5... Nf6 6. Qb3 Qe7 7. O-O { — 5... Nf6 abandons b7 and f7 at the same time; 6. Qb3! Qe7 7. O-O scores +1.17 with crushing dark-square pressure.<br><br>And against the blunder 5... Bc5??: } ) ( 5... Bc5 $4 6. Ne5 Bb4+ 7. Kf1 { — 5... Bc5?? walks straight into 6. Ne5!, +3.58 to +4.07 — f7 collapses immediately! } ) 6. O-O { 6. O-O tucks our king away, connects the f1-rook onto the d1-f1 battery, and prepares to meet ...Be6 with Bxe6 or Qb3. } { [%csl Yg1] [%cal Bf1e1,Bf1d1] } 6... Be6 { 6... Be6 is played 90% in Masters and 73% online to neutralize our c4-bishop. Check how White punishes Black's three rarer move-6 tries: } ( 6... Bc5 7. Qb3 Qf6 8. Bg5 { — 6... Bc5?! leaves b7 undefended; 7. Qb3! hits f7 and b7 simultaneously, +0.57 to +0.65.<br><br>What if 6... Nf6?: } ) ( 6... Nf6 7. Qb3 Qe7 8. Na3 { — 6... Nf6 again walks into 7. Qb3! Qe7 8. Na3, +1.05, eyeing Nb5 and b7.<br><br>And if 6... Qf6?: } ) ( 6... Qf6 7. Bg5 Qg6 8. Nbd2 { — 6... Qf6?! loses time to 7. Bg5 Qg6 8. Nbd2, +0.64 while White builds rapid development. } ) 7. Bxe6 $1 { Why choose 7. Bxe6! over autopilot 7. Bb5? Both score roughly 57% online, but trading on e6 immediately shatters Black's pawn structure — creating an isolated e6-pawn and a semi-open f-file, strips Black of their light-squared bishop, and opens the a2-g8 diagonal for Qb3.<br><br>After forced 7... fxe6, we follow up immediately with queen pressure on e6 and b7 before developing Nbd2 and b3. } { [%csl Ye6,Rf7] [%cal Be6f7,Bd1b3] } 7... fxe6 8. Qb3 { 8. Qb3 forks e6 and b7, forcing 8... Qd7 — since 8... Qc8 drops control of the center and allows Ng5. } { [%csl Yb3,Re6,Rb7] [%cal Bb3e6,Bb3b7] } 8... Qd7 9. Qxb7 { We grab the b7-pawn on move 9 while Black's kingside pieces are still asleep on g8 and f8, forcing Black's rook onto b8 before Qa6. } { [%csl Yb7,Ra8] [%cal Bb7a8,Bb7c6] } 9... Rb8 10. Qa6 { 10. Qa6 steps out of danger, keeps an eye on c4 and a7, and awaits Black's kingside setup. } { [%csl Ya6,Re6] [%cal Bb1d2,Ba6c4] } 10... Nf6 { 10... Nf6 develops actively and pressures e4, 68% Masters. Notice Black's two main alternatives — 10... Nge7 and 10... Bd6: } ( 10... Nge7 11. Nbd2 Ng6 12. Ne1 { — 10... Nge7, Le Quang Liem vs Aronian, 2021 aims for ...Ng6-e5; 11. Nbd2 Ng6 12. Ne1 prepares f4 and Nd3, +0.19 to +0.23.<br><br>And if 10... Bd6 first?: } ) ( 10... Bd6 11. Nbd2 Nge7 12. Nb3 { — 10... Bd6 followed by ...Ne7 meets 12. Nb3, +0.28, directly pressuring the d4-pawn and preparing Qc4. } ) 11. Nbd2 { 11. Nbd2 develops harmoniously, unpins the b2-pawn from the b8-rook, and prepares to jump to c4 or b3 while paving the way for b3 and Ba3. } { [%csl Yd2] [%cal Gb2b3,Bc1a3,Bd2c4] } 11... Bd6 12. b3 { 12. b3! is the key positional anchor we wanted: it reinforces c4 for our queen or knight, clears a3 for Ba3 to challenge Black's strong d6-bishop along the a3-f8 diagonal, and keeps an honest +0.20 to +0.28 structural edge.<br><br>━━━ PLAN FROM HERE ━━━<br><br>Verdict: +0.20 to +0.28 — Honest Practical Edge Against Black's Fractured e6-Pawn<br><br>• Our Plan — Play Ba3 after ...O-O to trade off Black's dark-squared d6-bishop, reroute Nd2-c4, and pressure e6 and d4.<br><br>• Watch Out — Black wants ...O-O, ...Rb6 to kick our queen, or ...e5 and ...Nh5-f4 to activate against our king; meet ...e5 with Ba3 and Qc4+.<br><br>• Key Trades & Endgame — Trading dark-squared bishops via Ba3 favors White because Black's remaining pawns on a7, c7, and e6 become fixed targets in a minor-piece or rook endgame.<br><br><br>━━━ SAMPLE CONTINUATIONS ━━━<br><br>Click either continuation below to see White's Ba3 pressure in action: } { [%csl Gb3,Re6,Ra7] [%cal Bc1a3,Bd2c4,Ba6c4,Rd4d3] } ( 12. b3 O-O 13. Ba3 { — 97% Masters reply: 13. Ba3! pins d6 to Rf8 and targets Black's dark squares, +0.24. } ) ( 12. b3 e5 13. Ba3 Rb6 14. Qa4 { — If Black tries 12... e5 to free the center, 13. Ba3 Rb6 14. Qa4 holds +0.34 with clean control over c4 and d6. } ) *

