{ When Black inserts 5... Bb4+ after capturing on d4, 6. Nbd2! keeps the bishop pair and all minor pieces on the board — scoring an impressive 65.7% online and 63.5% in Masters. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e4 e5 4. Nf3 exd4 5. Bxc4 Bb4+ 6. Nbd2 $1 { 6. Nbd2! beats routine 6. Bd2, +0.22 by keeping our dark-squared bishop and targeting Black's exposed b4-bishop and d4-pawn via a3 or Nb3. } { [%csl Yd2,Rb4] [%cal Bd2b3,Bc4f7] } ( 6. Bd2 Bxd2+ 7. Nbxd2 Nc6 { — Solid 6. Bd2, +0.22 to +0.35 simplifies dark-squared bishops cleanly, though 6. Nbd2, +0.44 preserves more dynamic winning chances. } ) 6... Nc6 7. O-O { 7. O-O shields our king and lines up the f1-rook on the f-file before we launch e4-e5. } { [%csl Yg1] [%cal Ge4e5,Bf1e1] } 7... Nf6 { 7... Nf6 develops toward e4 and prepares to meet e4-e5. Check Black's two alternatives — 7... Be7 and 7... Be6: } ( 7... Be7 8. Nb3 Nf6 9. Nbxd4 { — 7... Be7 retreats early and allows 8. Nb3 Nf6 9. Nbxd4 with smooth central centralization, +0.37.<br><br>And if 7... Be6?: } ) ( 7... Be6 8. Bxe6 fxe6 9. Qb3 { — 7... Be6 hands White the exact 8. Bxe6! fxe6 9. Qb3! motif from Line 1.1 — now even stronger at +0.86! } ) 8. e5 { 8. e5! kicks the f6-knight forward to d5 while gaining crucial kingside space and opening the d3-h7 diagonal for future play. } { [%csl Ge5,Rf6] [%cal Ge5f6,Bd2b3] } 8... Nd5 9. Nb3 { 9. Nb3 attacks the d4-pawn twice and clears the c1-h6 diagonal for our dark-squared bishop. } { [%csl Yb3,Rd4] [%cal Bb3d4,Bc4b5] } 9... Nb6 { 9... Nb6, played 86% in Masters kicks our c4-bishop while uncovering Black's queen against d4. If Black retreats 9... Be7 instead: } ( 9... Be7 10. Nbxd4 Nxd4 11. Qxd4 { — 9... Be7 allows the knight capture 10. Nbxd4 Nxd4 11. Qxd4, where White's centralized queen targets d5 and g7 at +0.31. } ) 10. Bb5 { 10. Bb5! pins the c6-knight to Black's king and keeps our bishop active instead of retreating passively to e2. } { [%csl Yb5,Rc6] [%cal Bb5c6,Bb3d4] } 10... Qd5 11. Nbxd4 { Capturing with 11. Nbxd4 wins back the d4-pawn cleanly with our knight while maintaining the pin on c6 and keeping the d-file open.<br><br>━━━ PLAN FROM HERE ━━━<br><br>Verdict: +0.26 to +0.36 — Balanced Dynamic Play With Active Minor Pieces<br><br>• Our Plan — Meet ...O-O or ...Bd7 with Bxc6 bxc6 Qc2, targeting the weakened c6/c7 queenside pawns and e5 space.<br><br>• Watch Out — Black plays ...O-O or ...Bd7 to break the pin and push ...c5; watch that d4-knight is protected against ...Bg4 and ...Bc5.<br><br>• Key Trades & Endgame — Trading Bxc6 bxc6 gives Black a doubled c-pawn target; keep queens on to exploit our kingside space, or play against the c-pawns in a middlegame.<br><br><br>━━━ SAMPLE CONTINUATIONS ━━━<br><br>Click either continuation below to see White's Bxc6+ Qc2 plan: } { [%csl Bd4,Yb5,Rc6] [%cal Bb5c6,Bd1c2,Bc1e3,Rc6d4] } ( 11. Nbxd4 O-O 12. Bxc6 bxc6 13. Qc2 { — 12. Bxc6! bxc6 damages Black's queenside structure before 13. Qc2 hits c6 and prepares Rd1, +0.26. } ) ( 11. Nbxd4 Bd7 12. Bxc6 bxc6 13. Qc2 { — If 11... Bd7, 12. Bxc6 bxc6 13. Qc2 still saddles Black with weak c-pawns at +0.71! } ) *

