{ Against early 4... Bb4+, we reject passive simplifications and choose 5. Nc3! — a completely sound response, +0.37 to +0.47 that creates immediate practical problems and scores ~60% in both online and Masters play. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e4 e5 4. Nf3 Bb4+ 5. Nc3 $1 { 5. Nc3! invites Black into open central tactics rather than trading dark-squared bishops with 5. Bd2. Why? Black's b4-bishop cannot stay active forever, and White gains rapid development with Nxd4, Bxc4, and f3. } { [%csl Yc3,Rb4] [%cal Bf1c4,Bf3e5] } 5... exd4 { 5... exd4 is played 76-80% of the time. Notice what happens if Black tries 5... Nf6 or greedy 5... b5: } ( 5... Nf6 6. Nxe5 b5 7. f3 { — 5... Nf6 leaves e5 undefended; 6. Nxe5! b5 7. f3 keeps a healthy central presence and cements e4 at +0.52.<br><br>And if Black tries 5... b5?: } ) ( 5... b5 $4 6. dxe5 Qxd1+ 7. Kxd1 { — 5... b5?? ignores the center; 6. dxe5! Qxd1+ 7. Kxd1, +1.17 leaves White clearly better in the endgame. } ) 6. Nxd4 { 6. Nxd4! is the soundest recapture — ahead of 6. Qxd4 +0.06, keeping queens on the board and opening the f3-square for f2-f3 later. } { [%csl Yd4] [%cal Bf1c4,Bd4f5] } 6... Nd7 { 6... Nd7 covers f6 and e5 cleanly. Check how White handles 6... Ne7 and 6... Qe7: } ( 6... Ne7 7. a3 Ba5 8. Be3 { — 6... Ne7 allows 7. a3! Ba5 8. Be3, +0.53, asking the bishop an immediate question before recapturing c4.<br><br>If 6... Qe7?: } ) ( 6... Qe7 7. Bxc4 Nf6 8. O-O { — 6... Qe7 gives White smooth development with 7. Bxc4 Nf6 8. O-O, +0.71 because 7... Qxe4+?? fails to 8. Kf1!, +1.51. } ) 7. Bxc4 { 7. Bxc4 recovers the pawn cleanly and aims directly at f7 along the open a2-g8 diagonal. } { [%csl Yc4,Rf7] [%cal Bc4f7,Be1g1] } 7... Ngf6 8. O-O { 8. O-O gets the king safe and prepares f2-f3 to solidify e4 against ...Ne5 or ...Nxe4. } { [%csl Yg1] [%cal Gf2f3,Bf1e1] } 8... O-O { 8... O-O is virtually forced; immediate 8... Bxc3?! runs into serious trouble: } ( 8... Bxc3 $2 9. bxc3 Ne5 10. Bb5+ { — 8... Bxc3 9. bxc3 hands White the uncontested bishop pair, and 9... Ne5 meets 10. Bb5+!, +2.62 disrupting Black's entire coordination. } ) 9. f3 { 9. f3 cements the e4-pawn permanently, denies ...Ng4 ideas, and clears Be3 and Qe2/Qc2 setups while keeping our active minor pieces.<br><br>━━━ PLAN FROM HERE ━━━<br><br>Verdict: +0.20 to +0.28 — Sound Practical Edge With Active Pieces & e4 Clamp<br><br>• Our Plan — Play Qe2 or Be3, tuck Bc4-b3 against ...Ne5, bring Rad1 into the open d-file, and later push a3 to challenge b4 or gain queenside space.<br><br>• Watch Out — Black plays ...Qe7 and ...Ne5 to attack c4, or ...Nb6 to hit c4; keep Bb3 ready and avoid letting Black trade light-squared bishops without gaining c3/d4 control.<br><br>• Key Trades & Endgame — Keep our light-squared c4-bishop alive on b3; if Black plays ...Bxc3 bxc3, our central pawn mass and bishop pair thrive in open middlegames.<br><br><br>━━━ SAMPLE CONTINUATIONS ━━━<br><br>Click either continuation below to see White's Bb3 and Be2 setups: } { [%csl Gf3,Ge4] [%cal Bc1g5,Bd4f5,Bf1e1,Rd7e5] } ( 9. f3 Qe7 10. Qe2 Ne5 11. Bb3 { — 57% Masters reply: 10. Qe2 defends e4 and c4 while 11. Bb3 preserves our prized bishop, +0.20. } ) ( 9. f3 Ne5 10. Be2 Qe7 11. Bg5 { — If 9... Ne5 immediately, 10. Be2 steps out calmly and prepares 11. Bg5 pinning f6, +0.26. } ) *

