{ Against the Alekhine System 3... Nf6, we push 4. e5! Nd5 5. Bxc4 Nb6 and choose practical 6. Bd3! over routine 6. Bb3. Why 6. Bd3? It scores 64.7% online and 59.5% in Masters, versus just 31.1% online for 6. Bb3, stops ...Bf5 cold, and builds a powerful f3 + Ne2 + Ne4 central clamp. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e4 Nf6 4. e5 { 4. e5! seizes space immediately and drives the f6-knight to d5. } { [%csl Ge5,Rf6] [%cal Ge5f6,Bf1c4] } 4... Nd5 5. Bxc4 { 5. Bxc4 recovers the pawn cleanly and prepares to challenge d5 or f7. } { [%csl Yc4,Rd5] [%cal Bc4d5,Gf2f4] } 5... Nb6 { 5... Nb6 attacks c4 with tempo. Watch two important alternatives here — the sound main option 5... Bf5 and solid 5... e6, 36.7% online: } ( 5... Bf5 6. Nc3 Nb6 7. e6 $1 Nxc4 8. Qa4+ Nc6 9. exf7+ Kxf7 10. Qxc4+ { — Firouzja vs Nakamura, 2025: 5... Bf5 is Stockfish's #1 alternative at +0.29 to +0.40, and Firouzja tested it sharply with 6. Nc3 Nb6 7. e6! — leading to a lively battle around +0.47 where Black must stay accurate after 7... Nxc4 8. Qa4+ Nc6 9. exf7+ Kxf7 10. Qxc4+.<br><br>What about solid 5... e6?: } ) ( 5... e6 6. Nc3 Bb4 7. Bd2 { — 5... e6, 36.7% online blocks c8; 6. Nc3 meets 6... Bb4, 44.6% online cleanly with 7. Bd2!, +0.86, unpinning and preparing Qg4! } ) 6. Bd3 $1 { 6. Bd3! is our practical weapon of choice over 6. Bb3. It claims the b1-h7 diagonal right away, stops ...Bf5 from activating, and clears c4 so our knights can step toward e4 without blocking. } { [%csl Yd3,Rh7] [%cal Bd3h7,Bg1e2] } 6... Nc6 { 6... Nc6 attacks d4 immediately and is played nearly 100% of the time. Notice two rarer tries — 6... g6 and 6... Be6: } ( 6... g6 7. Ne2 Bg7 8. O-O { — 6... g6 gives up central tension; 7. Ne2 Bg7 8. O-O keeps a comfortable +0.71 space edge.<br><br>If 6... Be6?: } ) ( 6... Be6 7. Nc3 Nc6 8. Be3 { — 6... Be6 leaves White with easy central development after 7. Nc3 Nc6 8. Be3, +0.72. } ) 7. Ne2 { 7. Ne2 defends d4 without blocking our f-pawn! Keeping f2-f3 available is the whole secret of the Bd3 bind — if Black plays ...Bg4, we simply kick it away and solidify e4. } { [%csl Ye2] [%cal Bb1c3,Gf2f3] } 7... Bg4 { 7... Bg4 pins e2 to our queen. Check Black's two rarer alternatives — 7... Be6 and 7... Nb4: } ( 7... Be6 8. Nbc3 Qd7 9. Ne4 { — 7... Be6 lets White jump straight to 8. Nbc3 Qd7 9. Ne4, +0.50 to +0.62 eyeing c5 and g5.<br><br>And if 7... Nb4?: } ) ( 7... Nb4 8. Be4 f5 9. exf6 { — 7... Nb4 8. Be4 f5?? is met by 9. exf6! exf6 10. Nf4, +1.87 with a fierce attack on the exposed king. } ) 8. f3 { 8. f3! is the positional anchor — it forces the g4-bishop to declare itself while reinforcing e5 and stopping ...Bg4 pins for good. } { [%csl Gf3,Rg4] [%cal Bb1c3,Bd3e4] } 8... Be6 { 8... Be6, 72% Masters choice eyes d5 and c4. Look at how we meet the older retreat 8... Bh5: } ( 8... Bh5 9. Be4 e6 10. Nbc3 { — Le Quang Liem vs Caruana, 2017: against 8... Bh5, 9. Be4! e6 10. Nbc3 builds a rock-solid central light-square bind, +0.42 to +0.52. } ) 9. Nbc3 { 9. Nbc3 develops toward e4 and protects d5-entry squares before Black castles queenside. } { [%csl Yc3] [%cal Bc3e4,Bc1e3] } 9... Qd7 10. Ne4 { 10. Ne4 centralizes the knight with threats of Nc5 — forking Qd7 and Be6 — while preparing Ne2-f4 or Be3, giving White a clean +0.47 to +0.59 positional edge.<br><br>━━━ PLAN FROM HERE ━━━<br><br>Verdict: +0.47 to +0.59 — Clear Positional Bind With Space Advantage<br><br>• Our Plan — Meet 10... Bd5 with 11. Nc5 Qc8 12. Bd2 or Ne2-f4, prepare O-O or Rc1, and expand on the queenside with a3/b4 or break with f4.<br><br>• Watch Out — Black plays ...Bd5 to block Nc5, ...Nb4 to hunt our d3-bishop, or ...O-O-O followed by ...f6; tuck Bd3 to b1 against ...Nb4 and watch d4.<br><br>• Key Trades & Endgame — Keep our d3-bishop unless Black trades on e4 or c4, and preserve the e5-pawn chain; minor-piece trades that leave Black's c8/e6/b6 complex cramped favor White.<br><br><br>━━━ SAMPLE CONTINUATIONS ━━━<br><br>Click either continuation below to see White's Nc5 and Bb1 responses: } { [%csl Be4,Gd4,Ge5] [%cal Be4c5,Bc1e3,Be2f4,Re6d5] } ( 10. Ne4 Bd5 11. Nc5 Qc8 12. Bd2 { — 93% Masters reply: 11. Nc5! forces the queen back to c8 and locks down dark squares, +0.59. } ) ( 10. Ne4 Nb4 11. Bb1 Bc4 12. Nc5 { — If 10... Nb4 chases our bishop, 11. Bb1! keeps the diagonal intact and still lands 12. Nc5, +0.73! } ) *

