n exchange on the spot to 9. Nb5!, +1.87 — after 9... O-O 10. Qxe7 Bxe7 11. Nc7, the a8-rook is trapped! } ) 9. h3 { 9. h3 is quiet prophylaxis — denying Black the g4-square before Nf3 develops, and preserving full central control. } { [%csl Gh3] [%cal Bg1f3,Ga2a4] } 9... h6 { 9... h6 stops Bg5. Check how White locks down Black's queenside against 9... a6: } ( 9... a6 10. a4 Bf5 11. Nf3 { — 9... a6 prepares ...b5; 10. a4! shuts down queenside counterplay immediately and holds +1.22. } ) 10. Nf3 { 10. Nf3 completes our kingside development at a crushing +1.54 advantage with Black's king stuck on f8 and rook trapped on h8.<br><br>━━━ PLAN FROM HERE ━━━<br><br>Verdict: +1.54 — Winning-Grade Strategic Edge Against Uncastled King<br><br>• Our Plan — Castle O-O, stop ...b5 with a4 whenever ...a6 appears, place Re1 or Bg5/Be3, and route Nc3-e4 against d6.<br><br>• Watch Out — Black desperately tries ...a6 and ...b5 or ...Bf5 and ...Nbd7 to activate; keep a4 ready and trade Black's good d6-bishop with Bd3 if useful.<br><br>• Key Trades & Endgame — With Black's h8-rook locked out of the game, every open-center exchange or queen-side bind favors White cleanly.<br><br><br>━━━ SAMPLE CONTINUATIONS ━━━<br><br>Click either continuation below to see White's O-O and Re1 clamp: } { [%csl Gd5,Rf8] [%cal Be1g1,Ga2a4,Bc1e3,Rc8f5] } ( 10. Nf3 a6 11. O-O b5 12. Bd3 { — SF19 #1: 11. O-O b5 12. Bd3, +1.54 retreats safely and prepares Re1 + Ne4! } ) ( 10. Nf3 Bf5 11. O-O Nbd7 12. Re1 { — Against 10... Bf5, 11. O-O and 12. Re1, +1.55 seize the e-file while h8 remains entombed. } ) *

